; Hermetic guard suite for the smbc model parser.
;
; Every case replays a canned smbc answer through the fake solver, so the suite
; pins what nunchaku makes of a model it cannot fully read -- something no run
; against a real smbc can exercise on demand, because the interesting answers
; are the ones smbc emits rarely or not at all.  What each case is for is
; spelled out case by case in README.md; the manifest both that file and these
; rules are written against is cases.tsv.
;
; The four expectation words, and what a golden must therefore show:
;   recovered  the unknown gets a type and prints as `?__<n>`
;   opaque     no type is determined, and the output keeps `?__ ?name`
;   conflict   one identity at two types: exactly one warning, and every
;              occurrence printed as `?__unparsable <ty>`
;   contained  the entry is lost and the rest of the model is delivered, rc=0
;
; The rules are generated: they are all the same shape and cases.tsv is the
; single table, so gen_dune.ml reads it and dune.inc is promoted from that.
(include dune.inc)

(rule
 (targets dune.inc.gen)
 (deps
  (:gen gen_dune.ml)
  cases.tsv)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   %{targets}
   (run ocaml %{gen}))))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff dune.inc dune.inc.gen)))

; The one case here that is not about the model parser.  It pins that nunchaku
; keeps passing --check to smbc, which is not a preference: smbc's --check
; gates Top_goals.check (), which is `assert false` when the model smbc just
; found does not satisfy the problem, and smbc's own default is NOT to check
; (its --help lists both --check and --no-check; invoking it with neither
; gives the unchecked answer).  On eleven of the seventy-nine captured
; Isabelle problems that assertion fires, and it is the only thing standing
; between nunchaku and a countermodel for a proposition that is true.
;
; The two replies are what real smbc 0.6.1 answers on this problem, captured
; verbatim: with --check it dies on the assertion with exit 2, and without it
; it returns `(result SAT :model ())` -- an EMPTY countermodel to a goal whose
; correct answer is UNSAT.  fake_solver picks between them by looking for
; --check on its own command line, so dropping the flag from Smbc.ml turns
; this case's golden from UNKNOWN into SAT and the diff fails loudly.
;
; Hand-written rather than a row in cases.tsv because it needs three
; environment variables no model-parser case has, and because its expectation
; is not one of that table's four words.
(rule
 (targets check_flag.out)
 (deps
  %{exe:../support/run_case.exe}
  %{exe:../support/fake_solver.exe}
  %{exe:../../src/main/nunchaku.exe}
  problems/inj_suc.nun
  models/no_check_sat.txt
  models/check_assert.txt)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   check_flag.out
   (run
    %{exe:../support/run_case.exe}
    --exe
    %{exe:../../src/main/nunchaku.exe}
    --deadline
    20
    --setenv
    "NUNCHAKU_SMBC=%{exe:../support/fake_solver.exe}"
    --setenv
    "FAKE_REPLIES=%{dep:models/no_check_sat.txt}"
    --setenv
    "FAKE_ON_ARG=--check"
    --setenv
    "FAKE_ON_ARG_REPLIES=%{dep:models/check_assert.txt}"
    --setenv
    "FAKE_ON_ARG_EXIT=2"
    --
    --solvers
    smbc
    --no-color
    -j
    1
    --timeout
    5
    %{dep:problems/inj_suc.nun}))))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff check_flag.expected check_flag.out)))
